DATA STRUCTURES & ALGORITHMS (DSA) — TOPIC-BY-TOPIC ROADMAP

1. Programming Basics & Complexity
- Language fundamentals: Syntax, loops, conditionals, functions, arrays/lists, and built-in library structures in C++, Java, or Python.
- Time & space complexity: Big O notation and how input size affects performance.

2. Arrays, Strings & Hashing
- Arrays & strings: Indexing, iteration, prefix sums, and string manipulation.
- Core patterns: Two pointers and sliding window.
- Hashing (hash maps / hash sets): Fast lookups and trading memory for speed.

3. Searching & Sorting
- Sorting: Bubble/selection/insertion basics, then merge sort, quick sort, and counting/radix sort.
- Searching: Linear search and binary search (including binary search on answers).

4. Linked Lists, Stacks & Queues
- Linked lists: Singly and doubly linked lists, pointer manipulation, and fast/slow pointers.
- Stacks & queues: LIFO/FIFO behavior, monotonic stacks, and deque basics.

5. Recursion & Backtracking
- Recursion: Base cases, call stacks, and divide-and-conquer thinking.
- Backtracking: Subsets, permutations, and constraint search.

Advanced topics needs to be covered 

6. Trees & Heaps
- Trees: Binary trees, traversals (in-order, pre-order, post-order, level-order), and binary search trees (BST).
- Heaps (priority queues): Min/max heaps and "top K" element problems.

7. Graphs & Greedy Algorithms
- Greedy algorithms: Local optimal choices, interval problems, and activity selection.
- Graphs: Adjacency lists/matrices, BFS, DFS, topological sort, shortest paths (Dijkstra, Bellman-Ford), and minimum spanning trees.

8. Dynamic Programming (DP) & Advanced Topics
- Dynamic programming: Memoization (top-down) and tabulation (bottom-up) across 1D, 2D, and common patterns (knapsack, LIS, grid paths).
- Advanced structures & techniques: Tries, Disjoint Set Union (DSU / union-find), segment trees / Fenwick trees, and bit manipulation.










DSA SYLLABUS & PRIORITY GUIDE: TCS (DIGITAL & PRIME) & INFOSYS (DSE & SP)

Overall Requirement: Roughly 75% to 85% of the 8-step DSA roadmap. You can skip heavy competitive programming structures (segment trees, Fenwick trees, advanced graph shortest paths) and focus heavily on arrays, hashing, greedy algorithms, and standard dynamic programming.

