2. TCS DIGITAL VS. TCS PRIME (TCS NQT ADVANCED CODING)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Exam Format: Both roles share the same coding paper; your score and code efficiency determine which tier you get shortlisted for.

- Must-Master Topics (Steps 1-4): Arrays (prefix sums, sliding window, two pointers), strings, hash maps for frequency counting, binary search, sorting, and stacks/queues.
- Prime Differentiators (Steps 5-8): Recursion, backtracking (subsets/permutations), binary tree traversals, BFS/DFS on grids, greedy interval problems, and standard 1D/2D DP (knapsack, coin change, LCS).
- What to Skip: Dijkstra/Bellman-Ford shortest paths, segment trees, Fenwick trees, tries, and advanced bitmask/digit DP.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
3. INFOSYS DSE VS. INFOSYS SPECIALIST PROGRAMMER (SP)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Exam Format: 3-hour coding test with 3 questions (Easy, Medium, Hard). Solving ~1.5 questions qualifies for DSE; solving 2 to 3 qualifies for SP.

- Question 1 - Easy (Steps 1-4 & 6): Arrays, strings, hashing, stacks (next greater element), basic trees, or math/bit manipulation.
- Question 2 - Medium (Step 7): Almost always Greedy algorithms (activity selection, interval scheduling, sorting-based optimization) or grid BFS/DFS.
- Question 3 - Hard (Step 8): Heavily favors Dynamic Programming (1D/2D DP, string DP, knapsack variations) combined with prefix sums or bitwise operations.
- What to Skip: Complex pointer-heavy linked list edge cases and niche structures like Fenwick trees or network flow.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
4. RECOMMENDED STUDY STRATEGY
---------------------------------------------------------------------
- For TCS Digital & Infosys DSE: Master Steps 1 through 4 completely, plus Greedy and basic 1D DP. Target 100-120 Easy/Medium problems.
- For TCS Prime & Infosys SP: Add Trees, Heaps, Graph BFS/DFS, and 2D DP. Target 180-220 problems with strict focus on O(N) or O(N log N) time complexity to pass hidden test cases.

